tuni (1.7.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The terminal core is Ghostty 1.4.0, which fixes a way for output to make
    the terminal write through freed memory (GHSA-w9q4-5jvv-mcv4). Searching
    50,000 lines of scrollback takes 25 ms instead of 45.
  * A frame lays out only the rows that changed, so a screen where a spinner
    moves costs half the CPU, and a closed pane or a flood of output hands its
    memory back.
  * Ctrl+Shift+J goes to the next tab that needs you, and a pasted screenshot
    arrives as the path of a file holding it.
  * The palette runs the checkout's tasks, opens a dev server's port, opens
    the checkout in Files or an editor, resumes a Claude Code conversation
    and opens a tab in a git worktree of its own.
  * Split panes without the keyboard are dimmed, the keyboard can follow the
    mouse, and a folder dropped on the sidebar opens as a project.

 -- Unisic <contact@unisic.app>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:40:00 +0100

tuni (1.7.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A host's files open in a pane beside this machine's, and a drag goes either
    way. Whole folders copy and delete, a remote file opens in the editor and
    saves back, and waiting transfers can be listed and cancelled. Sixty-four
    requests are in flight instead of one: 8 MiB over a link with 50 ms added
    comes down in 0.9 s instead of 16.6.
  * Hosts sit in groups and filter by a tag, a host can paint its panes in a
    theme of its own, and the host editor switches agent forwarding and
    environment variables. The connection settings are under Session in
    Preferences.
  * A snippet can leave blanks asked for before it runs, Ctrl+Shift+S opens the
    snippets alone, and a snippet can run on every host the list shows.
  * A dropped connection dials again when the network is back, a changed host
    key can be forgotten from the bar that reports it, a key can be made on a
    FIDO2 security key, and the "+" in the tab strip offers the last five hosts.
  * The shell starts about 5 ms after launch instead of 660, before GTK is
    loaded, and the window is up in a third of the time. A flood of output is
    read nearly a third faster, and PSS is a fifth lower than 1.6.0's. Idle
    panes wake the window together, so sixteen of them cost a quarter of the
    wakeups they did.
  * Fedora can take Tuni from COPR, and updates then arrive with the rest of
    the system.

 -- Unisic <contact@unisic.app>  Sun, 25 Oct 2026 21:20:00 +0100

tuni (1.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A host can keep its password, in the desktop keyring through secret-tool.
    ssh reads it through a helper, only for a host whose key is already known;
    Tuni never reads it back. The Keys dialog takes a private key pasted from
    elsewhere and puts it in ~/.ssh.
  * The host editor is laid out in cards, and a host can have an icon, with
    forty-two system logos to choose from.
  * The mouse wheel over the tab strip moves one tab per notch, and a tab
    dropped on the desktop becomes a window on Wayland too.
  * A new tab's shell starts at the size of its pane, so the prompt no longer
    folds onto itself with the cursor in the middle of it.
  * Discord Rich Presence: the file or program, the repository, its branch and
    a button to its remote. Off until turned on, with a switch per part.
  * UsageFleet's group budget: the 5 hour and weekly share this device's group
    has spent, beside Claude Code and Pi, and when the group refuses prompts.
    Asked once a minute at most; a switch under Agents to Recognise.

 -- Unisic <contact@unisic.app>  Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:30:00 +0200

tuni (1.5.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A launch that names no directory opens a window again. Since 1.5.0 it only
    raised the window already open, so a desktop shortcut bound to Ctrl+Alt+T
    did nothing once Tuni was running. A launch that names a folder still
    opens that folder in the window already running.
  * An AI Agents page of switches: whether the agents' session logs are read
    at all, which agents count as one, whether Claude Code's plan bars are
    asked for over the network, whether a tab spins while a turn runs, and
    whether a turn that ends out of sight leaves a mark, raises a notification
    or rings the bell.
  * Pi is read like the other three agents. Its title says nothing about a
    turn running, so it gets no spinner of its own; data/pi carries an
    extension that makes it say so, installed with "pi install".

 -- Unisic <contact@unisic.app>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 22:05:00 +0200

tuni (1.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Tuni opens on a fresh shell rather than on the window that was left.
    Restoring a session is a switch now and it is off, and a second switch
    decides whether a restored pane starts in the directory its shell was
    working in. That directory is read from the kernel when a shell does not
    send OSC 7, which is most of them.
  * A tab dragged off the strip is a move rather than a loss: onto the desktop
    it becomes a window of its own, onto another Tuni window's strip it joins
    that window, and onto a project row it moves into that project. A
    project's row dragged off the sidebar moves everything in it at once.
  * `Ctrl+Shift+R` reopens the tab just closed, and the four closed before it,
    with its panes, the room they had, the name that was typed and what each
    terminal had printed.
  * A tab spins while a coding agent is thinking in it and the project's row
    spins in place of its folder, and a command that ran ten seconds or longer
    raises a notification when it exits in a tab that is out of sight.
  * `Ctrl+Shift+Page Up` and `Ctrl+Shift+Page Down` walk the scrollback one
    prompt at a time, `Ctrl+Alt+I` sends what is typed to every pane of the tab
    at once, and `Ctrl+Shift+Backspace` wipes a half-typed command whole.
  * A project's row can carry an icon or an emoji in place of the folder.
  * Opening a terminal from a file manager reaches the Tuni that is already
    running and opens a project rooted at the folder that was chosen, rather
    than a second copy of Tuni that ignores the directory.
  * Every preferences row says what it does in one line, and what a new tab
    opens, which shell runs and how much of a session comes back are a Session
    page of their own.

 -- Unisic <contact@unisic.app>  Wed, 05 Aug 2026 19:00:00 +0200

tuni (1.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The install command in the README runs in fish, which has no process
    substitution: it is a pipe into bash rather than bash reading a
    substituted file.
  * The build instructions name every package a build needs, and the four
    more that `make check` wants.

 -- Unisic <contact@unisic.app>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 14:30:00 +0200

tuni (1.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Tuni checks whether a newer Tuni has been released, once per run, and
    offers an Update button that runs the installer in a tab where sudo can
    ask for a password. Preferences, Terminal, Updates turns the check off.
  * scripts/install.sh opens a menu: what is installed, install or update,
    install an older version from the release page, or remove Tuni with or
    without its settings. It installs the package built for the distribution
    it runs on.
  * A project can opt out of starting a new tab where the visible shell is,
    while the rest of them go on following it.
  * A translucent window repaints whole, so a sliver of text no longer
    survives on chrome with nothing opaque under it.
  * A glyph whose ink reaches past its cell is clipped to the grid instead of
    landing on the tab bar.

 -- Unisic <contact@unisic.app>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0200

tuni (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The scrollback keeps the number of lines the setting names, where a
    ten-thousand-line setting used to hold about nine hundred rows.
  * An SFTP transfer shows percent and speed and can be cancelled, and a
    cancelled transfer leaves no half file at either end.
  * The Info page opens the project in an installed code editor.
  * A pane falls back to xterm-256color where xterm-ghostty has no terminfo.
  * The window chrome: a commit graph on the Git page, a sticky New Project
    under the last project, solid dialogs under a translucent window, and a
    pane grip that lights only under the pointer.
  * The window goes quiet when it is not active: the panel and diff polls skip
    their tick, the scrollbar fade redraws only when it moved, and the live
    search re-runs on the frame clock. The Info page's editor icons are looked
    up when the page opens rather than before the first frame.
  * A closed pane lets go of its handlers, its replay text and its textures,
    and a git child whose stdin write failed is reaped and reports what git
    itself said.

 -- Unisic <contact@unisic.app>  Wed, 29 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0200

tuni (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Clicks, drags and the wheel behave the way Ghostty's do, and a URL that
    is only text opens with Ctrl+click.
  * A copy is confirmed with a toast, including one an application makes over
    OSC 52.
  * The editor drives a language server and a debugger, and a selection grows
    by syntax with Alt+Up. The settings choose the panel's pages and the
    window's shortcuts.
  * The window blur asks X11 as well as Wayland, and faint text draws halfway
    to the background.
  * Escape closes the command palette, and a taken forward port names the
    process that actually holds the address.

